1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
African Bitcoin businesses and communities pay
contractors and contributors in sats.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,090
The approval is a chat message.

3
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:11,000
The payment is a wallet screen. The proof is a
screenshot.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
When the wallet's answer is lost mid-payment,
nobody knows whether the money moved.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
So people retry and pay twice, or wait and
leave someone unpaid.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Orynt sits in front of a wallet you already
run. It never holds your funds.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
It decides who may pay, makes sure a payment
is sent once, and keeps the proof.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,182
Everything you will see is a simulation.

9
00:00:36,182 --> 00:00:40,000
The wallet is a labelled simulator: no Bitcoin
network, no real money.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,333
A maker enters a payout for Bob.

11
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:47,000
Orynt decodes his invoice and binds the
amount, node and expiry.

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The default policy needs one approver for
30,000 sats, and two above 100,000.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The maker cannot approve their own payout.
Refused.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,755
The approver signs the exact payment with
their own key.

15
00:01:04,755 --> 00:01:09,000
Change any field and the signature stops
matching.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Roles are checked again at execution, not just
at approval.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,392
Execute. Orynt first reserves a single-use
intent in PostgreSQL,

18
00:01:19,392 --> 00:01:22,000
then asks the simulated wallet to pay.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The simulator returns a preimage, the way a
real wallet would.

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,085
Orynt checks the preimage hashes to the
invoice.

21
00:01:35,085 --> 00:01:39,000
The state is simulated-settled, never settled.

22
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,034
A real Lightning adapter exists in the code,

23
00:01:42,034 --> 00:01:45,000
but it has not yet been run against a node.

24
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,417
Now the failure that causes double payments.

25
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:53,000
The simulator accepts the payment, then drops
the response.

26
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Orynt does not call it failed. The state is
unknown, and sending again is blocked.

27
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Someone presses execute again. Refused: the
first attempt may already have paid.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,443
Reconciliation asks the wallet for its own
record by payment hash.

29
00:02:12,443 --> 00:02:15,000
Settled. One send, one payment.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,387
Orynt issues a signed receipt: the agreement,

31
00:02:18,387 --> 00:02:22,000
the approvals, the preimage and the audit
chain.

32
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,355
A standalone command checks it offline.

33
00:02:25,355 --> 00:02:30,000
The verdict is verified-simulated: not a real
payment.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Now we change a single character of the
receipt. Rejected.

35
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,213
Next: more wallet adapters, mobile-first
approvals,

36
00:02:43,213 --> 00:02:48,000
and interviews with African Bitcoin teams to
test whether this pain is real.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,593
Limits today: regtest only, application-level
enforcement,

38
00:02:51,593 --> 00:02:55,000
and an audit log that is tamper-evident, not
immutable.

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,500
Orynt. Every payment, under control.
